   <content>
      <section class="practice" id="sect-00001">
         <para id="para-00001">Complete the following questions to practice the skills you have learned in this
            lesson.
        </para>

            <exercise id="_15b7b257-480b-4055-a9e9-6ce79260cafc" class="multiple-choice">
               <problem id="prob-00001">
                  <para id="para-00002">Xander is loading a pallet with boxes that each weighs 45 pounds. The
                     pallet can safely support no more than 900 pounds. How many boxes can he safely
                     load onto the pallet?____ boxes or less</para>
               </problem>
               <solution id="sol-00001">
                  <para id="para-00003">Correct! 20</para>
                  <para id="para-00004">Try again. Revisit activity 2.10.2.</para>
                  <para id="para-00005">The correct answer is 20. Each box weighs 45 pounds. The total amount of
                     weight the pallet can support is 900 pounds. Each box weighs 45 pounds. The
                     total amount of weight the pallet can support is 900 pounds. Let <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>b</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>
                     represent the number of boxes that can safely be loaded onto the pallet. Let
                     <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>b</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> represent the number of boxes that can safely be loaded onto the pallet.
                     The equation is : 45b≤900 The equation is : 45b≤900 Solve for <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>b</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> : b≤20
                     Solve for <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>b</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> : b≤20 The number of boxes that can be safely loaded onto the
                     pallet is less than or equal to 20. The number of boxes that can be safely
                     loaded onto the pallet is less than or equal to 20.</para>
               </solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise id="_2fa05935-8dc2-4217-8896-72f9156be9f1" class="multiple-choice">
               <problem id="prob-00002">
                  <para id="para-00006">The elevator in Yehire’s apartment building has a sign that says the
                     maximum weight is 2100 pounds. If the average weight of one person is 150
                     pounds, how many people can safely ride the elevator? a maximum of ___ people</para>
               </problem>
               <solution id="sol-00002">
                  <para id="para-00007">Correct! 14</para>
                  <para id="para-00008">Try again. Revisit activity 2.10.2.</para>
                  <para id="para-00009">The correct answer is 14. An average person's weight is 150 pounds. The
                     total amount of weight the elevator can hold is 2100 pounds. An average
                     person's weight is 150 pounds. The total amount of weight the elevator can hold
                     is 2100 pounds. Let <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>p</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> represent the number of people that can safely ride
                     the elevator at one time. Let <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>p</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> represent the number of people that can
                     safely ride the elevator at one time. The equation is: 150p≤2100 Solve for
                     <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>p</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>: p≤14 The number of people that can safely ride the elevator at one time
                     is less than or equal to 14. The equation is: 150p≤2100 Solve for <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>p</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>: p≤14
                     The number of people that can safely ride the elevator at one time is less than
                     or equal to 14.</para>
               </solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise id="_48ad04c3-a04c-4f81-b9f5-5e7b0acdff38" class="multiple-choice">
               <problem id="prob-00003">
                  <para id="para-00010">Jaden is looking at apartments with three of his friends. They want the
                     monthly rent to be no more than $2360. If the roommates split the rent evenly
                     among the four of them, what is the maximum rent each will pay? $____</para>
               </problem>
               <solution id="sol-00003">
                  <para id="para-00011">Correct! 590</para>
                  <para id="para-00012">Try again. Revisit activity 2.10.2.</para>
                  <para id="para-00013">The correct answer is 590. There are 4 people that want to rent an apartment
                     together. The total amount they want to pay for rent per month is $2360. There
                     are 4 people that want to rent an apartment together. The total amount they
                     want to pay for rent per month is $2360. Let <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>r</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> represent the amount of rent
                     each person will pay. Let <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>r</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> represent the amount of rent each person will
                     pay. The equation is: 4r≤$2360 The equation is: 4r≤$2360 Solve for <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>r</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>:
                     r≤$590 Solve for <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>r</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>: r≤$590 Each of the 4 people will pay a maximum of $590
                     per month for rent to stay under the total amount of $2360. Each of the 4
                     people will pay a maximum of $590 per month for rent to stay under the total
                     amount of $2360.</para>
               </solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise id="_7d241ae8-5bad-435e-8aef-eb5e84781416" class="multiple-choice">
               <problem id="prob-00004">
                  <para id="para-00014">Melody got a $20 gift card for the coffee shop. Her favorite iced drink
                     costs $3.79. What is the maximum number of drinks she can buy with the gift
                     card? ____ drinks</para>
               </problem>
               <solution id="sol-00004">
                  <para id="para-00015">Correct! 5</para>
                  <para id="para-00016">Try again. Revisit activity 2.10.2.</para>
                  <para id="para-00017">The correct answer is 5.</para>
                  <para id="para-00018">Each drink costs $3.79. The total amount she has to spend is $20. Let <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>d</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>
                     represent the number of drinks that can be purchased using the gift card. Let
                     <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>d</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> represent the number of drinks that can be purchased using the gift card.
                     The equation is: $3.79d≤$20 The equation is: $3.79d≤$20 Solve for <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>d</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>: d≤5.28
                     Solve for <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>d</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>: d≤5.28 She can buy a maximum of 5 drinks, which would cost
                     $3.79 x 5 = $18.95. That would leave $20 - $18.95 = $1.05 on the gift card,
                     which is not enough to buy a 6th drink. She can buy a maximum of 5 drinks,
                     which would cost $3.79 x 5 = $18.95. That would leave $20 - $18.95 = $1.05 on
                     the gift card, which is not enough to buy a 6th drink.</para>
               </solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise id="_39730d15-37e4-4f6b-90f3-cd46b7260741" class="multiple-choice">
               <problem id="prob-00005">
                  <para id="para-00019">Teegan likes to play golf. He has budgeted $60 next month for the driving
                     range. It costs him $10.55 for a bucket of balls each time he goes. What is the
                     maximum number of times he can go to the driving range next month? ____ times</para>
               </problem>
               <solution id="sol-00005">
                  <para id="para-00020">Correct! 5</para>
                  <para id="para-00021">Try again. Revisit activity 2.10.2.</para>
                  <para id="para-00022">The correct answer is 5.</para>
                  <para id="para-00023">Each bucket of balls costs $10.55. His total budget for the month is $60.
                     Let <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>n</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> represent the number of times he can visit the driving range without
                     going over his budget. Let <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>n</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> represent the number of times he can visit the
                     driving range without going over his budget. The equation is: $10.55n≤$60 The
                     equation is: $10.55n≤$60 Solve for <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>n</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>: n≤5.69 Solve for <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>n</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>: n≤5.69 He can
                     go to the range 5 times, which would cost $10.55 x 5 = $52.75. He would be
                     under budget by $60 - $52.75 = $7.25, which is not enough money for a 6th trip
                     to the range. He can go to the range 5 times, which would cost $10.55 x 5 =
                     $52.75. He would be under budget by $60 - $52.75 = $7.25, which is not enough
                     money for a 6th trip to the range.</para>
               </solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise id="_55b9d39b-3707-4035-b44f-34aee7584723" class="multiple-choice">
               <problem id="prob-00006">
                  <para id="para-00024">Ryan charges his neighbors $17.50 to wash their car. How many cars must
                     he wash next summer if his goal is to earn at least $1500? ____ cars</para>
               </problem>
               <solution id="sol-00006">
                  <para id="para-00025">Correct! 86</para>
                  <para id="para-00026">Try again. Revisit activity 2.10.2.</para>
                  <para id="para-00027">The correct answer is 86.</para>
                  <para id="para-00028">He charges $17.50 for each wash. He wants to make at least $1500. Let <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>c</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>
                     represent the number of cars he has to wash to make at least $1500. Let <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>c</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>
                     represent the number of cars he has to wash to make at least $1500. The
                     equation is: $17.50c≤$1500 The equation is: $17.50c≤$1500 Solve for <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>c</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>:
                     c≤85.71 Solve for <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>c</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>: c≤85.71 He has to wash 86 cars to make at least $1500.
                     If he washes 85 cars he will make 85($17.50)=$1487.50, which is lower than his
                     goal. If he washes 86 cars he will make 86($17.50)=$1505, which is higher than
                     his goal. He has to wash 86 cars to make at least $1500. If he washes 85 cars
                     he will make 85($17.50)=$1487.50, which is lower than his goal. If he washes 86
                     cars he will make 86($17.50)=$1505, which is higher than his goal.</para>
               </solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise id="_3973a4a2-3e42-4e3e-9bdb-7902568e1d43" class="multiple-choice">
               <problem id="prob-00007">
                  <para id="para-00029">Alejandro’s water bill is $24.80 per month plus $2.20 per ccf (hundred
                     cubic feet) of water. What is the maximum number of ccf Alejandro can use if he
                     wants his bill to be no more than $60? ____ ccf</para>
               </problem>
               <solution id="sol-00007">
                  <para id="para-00030">Correct! 16</para>
                  <para id="para-00031">Try again. Revisit activity 2.10.2.</para>
                  <para id="para-00032">The correct answer is 16. He pays a flat monthly fee of $24.80 plus an
                     additional cost of $2.20 per ccf that he uses each month. He wants his bill to
                     be no more than $60. He pays a flat monthly fee of $24.80 plus an additional
                     cost of $2.20 per ccf that he uses each month. He wants his bill to be no more
                     than $60. Let <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>w</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> represent the amount of water he uses each month. Let <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>w</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>
                     represent the amount of water he uses each month. The equation is:
                     $24.80+$2.20w≤$60 The equation is: $24.80+$2.20w≤$60 Solve for <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>w</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>:
                     $2.20w≤35.20 Solve for <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>w</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>: $2.20w≤35.20 Solve for <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>w</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>: w≤16 Solve for
                     <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>w</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>: w≤16 He can use less than or equal to 16 ccf of water per month and keep
                     his bill under $60. He can use less than or equal to 16 ccf of water per month
                     and keep his bill under $60.</para>
               </solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise id="_5b8372d5-500a-451c-8414-3a6ed0920e4c" class="multiple-choice">
               <problem id="prob-00008">
                  <para id="para-00033">Look at the inequality <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mi>m</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>6</m:mn><m:mo>&lt;</m:mo><m:mn>14</m:mn><m:mi>−</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mi>m</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>. Which of the following is
                     NOT true?</para>
                  <para id="para-00034">Multiple Choice:</para>
                  <para id="para-00035">If <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>m</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> is a negative number, then <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mi>m</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>6</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> could be a positive or
                     negative number.</para>
                  <para id="para-00036">If <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>m</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> is a negative number, then <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>14</m:mn><m:mi>−</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mi>m</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> will always be positive.</para>
                  <para id="para-00037">If <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>m</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> is a positive number, then <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mi>m</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>6</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> will always be positive.</para>
                  <para id="para-00038">If <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>m</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> is a negative number, then <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>14</m:mn><m:mi>−</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mi>m</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> will always be negative.</para>
               </problem>
               <solution id="sol-00008">
                  <para id="para-00039">Correct! If m is a negative number, then <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>14</m:mn><m:mi>−</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mi>m</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> will always be negative</para>
                  <para id="para-00040">Try again. Revisit activity 2.10.3</para>
                  <para id="para-00041">The correct answer is if <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>m</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> is a negative number, then <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>14</m:mn><m:mi>−</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mi>m</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> will
                     always be negative. Substitute in -1 and -2 for <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>m</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> to evaluate statement a.
                     Substitute in -1 and -2 for <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>m</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> to evaluate statement a. <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mi>m</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>6</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mi>m</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>6</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>
                     <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>6</m:mn><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>6</m:mn><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>6</m:mn><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>6</m:mn><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>6</m:mn><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>10</m:mn><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>6</m:mn><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>6</m:mn><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>10</m:mn><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>6</m:mn><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>
                     Statement a is true because when <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>m</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> is -1, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mi>m</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>6</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> is positive, but any
                     negative number smaller than -1, will make it negative, so <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mi>m</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>6</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> could be a
                     positive or negative number. Statement a is true because when <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>m</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> is -1,
                     <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mi>m</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>6</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> is positive, but any negative number smaller than -1, will make it
                     negative, so <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mi>m</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>6</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> could be a positive or negative number. Substitute in -1
                     for <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>m</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> to evaluate statement b. Substitute in -1 for <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>m</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> to evaluate
                     statement b. <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>14</m:mn><m:mi>−</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mi>m</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>14</m:mn><m:mi>−</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mi>m</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>14</m:mn><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>14</m:mn><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>16</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>14</m:mn><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>14</m:mn><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>16</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>
                     Statement b is true because when <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>m</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> is -1, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>14</m:mn><m:mi>−</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mi>m</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> is positive, and any
                     negative number would make the expression positive because subtracting a
                     negative number always results in a positive number. Statement b is true
                     because when <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>m</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> is -1, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>14</m:mn><m:mi>−</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mi>m</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> is positive, and any negative number would
                     make the expression positive because subtracting a negative number always
                     results in a positive number. Substitute in 1 for <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>m</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> to evaluate statement
                     c. Substitute in 1 for <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>m</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> to evaluate statement c. <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mi>m</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>6</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mi>m</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>6</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>
                     <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>6</m:mn><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>6</m:mn><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>11</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>6</m:mn><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>6</m:mn><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>11</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> Statement c is true. If <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>m</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> is a positive
                     number, then any other positive number will make 5m+6 positive number.
                     Statement c is true. If <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>m</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> is a positive number, then any other positive
                     number will make 5m+6 positive number. Because statement b is true for If <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>m</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>
                     is a negative number, then <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>14</m:mn><m:mi>−</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mi>m</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> will always be positive, statement d has
                     to be false because <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>14</m:mn><m:mi>−</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mi>m</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> can not be a negative number when <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>m</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> is
                     negative. Because statement b is true for If <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>m</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> is a negative number, then
                     <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>14</m:mn><m:mi>−</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mi>m</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> will always be positive, statement d has to be false because
                     <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>14</m:mn><m:mi>−</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mi>m</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> can not be a negative number when <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>m</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> is negative. Statement d is
